From: Robin <robin@localhost>
Subject: [PATCH] WebCore compositing: a blur or drop-shadow filter gets its own layer with coordinated graphics

WebChromeClient leaves FilterTrigger out of the compositing triggers, so an element with `filter: blur()` that is not
composited for another reason is filtered on the CPU, on the web process's main thread, at every repaint. On the TV one
animated blurred element kept that thread at 100% (SkBlurEngine under RenderLayer::applyFilters) and every tab stopped
answering. Robin worked around it in compat.js by walking every CSS rule of every page and adding `will-change: filter`
where a rule had a blur; that walk cost ~1-2 s per page load on the TV. With coordinated graphics the compositor applies
any filter on the GPU, so a filter that moves pixels (blur, drop-shadow) now makes the element composited directly.
Backdrop filters were already composited; url() reference filters are left alone (never composited).
---
diff --git a/Source/WebCore/rendering/RenderLayerCompositor.cpp b/Source/WebCore/rendering/RenderLayerCompositor.cpp
--- a/Source/WebCore/rendering/RenderLayerCompositor.cpp
+++ b/Source/WebCore/rendering/RenderLayerCompositor.cpp
@@ -3998,8 +3998,18 @@
         return true;
 #endif
 
-    if (!(m_compositingTriggers & ChromeClient::FilterTrigger))
+    if (!(m_compositingTriggers & ChromeClient::FilterTrigger)) {
+#if USE(COORDINATED_GRAPHICS)
+        // A blur or a drop shadow on a layer that is not composited is redrawn on the CPU, on the web process's main thread,
+        // at every repaint of the element; one animated blurred element kept that thread at 100% (TV, LG CX). The coordinated
+        // compositor applies any filter on the GPU, so such an element gets its own layer, as `will-change: filter` would.
+        // Filters that do not move pixels (grayscale, brightness, ...) stay where they are: they are cheap to paint, and
+        // url() reference filters are never applied by the compositor, so a layer would not help them.
+        return renderer.hasFilter() && renderer.style().filter().hasFilterThatMovesPixels() && !renderer.style().filter().hasReferenceFilter();
+#else
         return false;
+#endif
+    }
 
     return renderer.hasFilter();
 }
